<p>
I live in Emacs, and usually only restart it when absolutely necessary. When I do restart, I occasionally get the dreaded message that there&rsquo;s something wrong with my init file. I&rsquo;m then prompted to start with the <code>--debug-init</code> switch, which I rarely find helpful. It&rsquo;s been my experience that the error is often from having unbalanced parens somewhere, and I&rsquo;ve always found them by brute force&mdash;commenting out half of the file, restarting, commenting out another half, restarting, etc. This is a tedious job with an init file that is thousands of lines long.
</p>

<p>
Recently, I stumbled on the <code>check-parens</code> function. Run it from the beginning of the buffer, and it will stop at the outermost open paren that contains the error. I don&rsquo;t know how many hours this would have saved me in the past, but better late than never.
</p>

